BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Quantum error correction: from its beginnings to modern developmen
 ts — Eleanor Rieffel (University of Queensland)
DTSTART:20260814T010000Z
DTEND:20260814T020000Z
DTSTAMP:20260821T144100Z
UID:17688-20260814@uq-seminar-calendar
SEQUENCE:0
CREATED:20260821T144100Z
DESCRIPTION:Shor’s polynomial-time quantum algorithm for factoring is wi
 dely credited as moving quantum computing from an intriguing but obscure p
 ossibility to a field of mainstream interest. Without the introduction of 
 quantum error correction\, thought by many leading figures to be impossibl
 e\, Shor’s algorithm would have remained a mathematical curiosity. Quant
 um error correction is now one of the most mature areas of quantum informa
 tion processing\, while still a vibrant area of research in which major br
 eakthroughs continue to be made. This talk will begin with an introduction
  to quantum error correction\, including some history that frames evolving
  views on quantum error correction. I will start by defining stabilizer co
 des\, providing an overview of the powerful stabilizer formalism. I will m
 ove from stabilizer codes to subsystem codes\, defining them generally and
  giving the Bacon-Shor subsystem codes as an example. The last part of the
  talk will discuss a much newer type of code\, dynamical or Floquet\, code
 s\, providing a glimpse of the Floquet Bacon-Shor code\, discovered in joi
 nt work with M. Sohaib Alam. This work is part of a larger program aiming 
 to understand the design space for Floquet codes. The talk will conclude w
 ith some open questions.\n\nhttps://smp.uq.edu.au/event/session/17688
LAST-MODIFIED:20260821T144100Z
LOCATION:Parnell Building (07)\, Room: 222
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Why is the strong force strong? What topology tells us about quark
  interactions. — Prof Chris Allton (Swansea)
DTSTART:20260807T010000Z
DTEND:20260807T020000Z
DTSTAMP:20260821T144100Z
UID:17687-20260807@uq-seminar-calendar
SEQUENCE:0
CREATED:20260821T144100Z
DESCRIPTION:The strong force between quarks has an intriguing nature. In m
 ost of the universe\, it is strongly confining and quarks are bound by glu
 ons into protons and other hadrons. This “confinement” property underp
 ins the stability of matter. However\, just after the Big Bang\, the stron
 g interaction was in a high temperature\, deconfined\, regime. Understandi
 ng the cause of this confinement-deconfinement transition is one of the ce
 ntral\, unsolved problems in particle physics. A compelling description of
  confinement can be constructed using “centre vortices”\, topological 
 structures in the gluon fields analogous to the twist in a Möbius strip. 
 Using this approach we’ve revealed three “phases” of the strong forc
 e as the temperature increases with deconfinement only occurring in the ho
 ttest phase. We present novel ideas using topology to build a deeper under
 standing of confinement.\n\nhttps://smp.uq.edu.au/event/session/17687
LAST-MODIFIED:20260821T144100Z
LOCATION:Physiology Lecture Theatres Room: 348
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Supernova cosmology with Euclid\, Rubin and TiDES — Professor Is
 obel Hook (Lancaster University)
DTSTART:20260724T010000Z
DTEND:20260724T020000Z
DTSTAMP:20260821T144100Z
UID:17659-20260724@uq-seminar-calendar
SEQUENCE:0
CREATED:20260821T144100Z
DESCRIPTION:In this talk I will discuss recent results and upcoming prospe
 cts for observing distant supernovae. I will begin with a brief review of 
 previous supernova surveys and the use of Type Ia supernovae in cosmology.
  Recent results suggest that the properties of dark energy may be changing
  with time\, in contrast to the cosmological constant explanation for dark
  energy. This provides new impetus for improving our measurements of the c
 osmological parameters. I will describe prospects for supernova observatio
 ns from the Time Domain Extragalactic Survey (TiDES) which will use the 4M
 OST instrument to obtain spectra tens of thousands of supernovae and their
  host galaxies discovered by the Rubin Observatory's Legacy Survey of Spac
 e and Time (LSST). I will also describe some early results and prospects f
 or supernova science with the Euclid mission\, and discuss how the combina
 tion of Rubin/LSST\, TiDES and Euclid will advance supernova cosmology.\n\
 nhttps://smp.uq.edu.au/event/session/17659
LAST-MODIFIED:20260821T144100Z
LOCATION:Parnell Building (07)\, Room: 222
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Label-free and quantum imaging — Professor Irina Kabakova (Unive
 rsity of Technology Sydney)
DTSTART:20260529T010000Z
DTEND:20260529T020000Z
DTSTAMP:20260821T144100Z
UID:17542-20260529@uq-seminar-calendar
SEQUENCE:0
CREATED:20260821T144100Z
DESCRIPTION:Imaging for biological and biomedical applications is entering
  a new era of label-free detection to eliminate possible toxicity and phot
 odamage to tissues and cells from the use of labels or powerful light sour
 ces. Brillouin imaging is gaining popularity as all-optical elastography t
 echnique with microscopic resolution for micromechanical characterisation 
 of biomaterials. Additionally\, imaging using nonclassical states of light
 \, e.g. entangled photons\, also growing in popularity as the means to imp
 rove spatial resolution or reach to the new frequency bands. In this prese
 ntation\, I will talk about Brillouin and quantum imaging - the two imagin
 g modalities developed in my lab with the focus on specific problems in bi
 omedical field such as cancer diagnostics.\n\nhttps://smp.uq.edu.au/event/
 session/17542
LAST-MODIFIED:20260821T144100Z
LOCATION:Hawken Engineering Building (50)\, Room: N202
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Quantum Communication: How Physics Can Secure Information — Dr. 
 Josephine Dias (University of Queensland)
DTSTART:20260522T010000Z
DTEND:20260522T020000Z
DTSTAMP:20260821T144100Z
UID:17541-20260522@uq-seminar-calendar
SEQUENCE:0
CREATED:20260821T144100Z
DESCRIPTION:Quantum communication uses the laws of quantum mechanics to se
 nd information in ways that are fundamentally more secure than classical m
 ethods. In this talk\, we’ll explore how photons and entanglement enable
  tasks like quantum key distribution\, and why loss and noise are central 
 challenges.\n\nhttps://smp.uq.edu.au/event/session/17541
LAST-MODIFIED:20260821T144100Z
LOCATION:Hawken Engineering Building (50)\, Room: N202
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Modelling large-scale brain dynamics — Associate Professor James
  Roberts (QIMR Berghofer)
DTSTART:20260515T010000Z
DTEND:20260515T020000Z
DTSTAMP:20260821T144100Z
UID:17540-20260515@uq-seminar-calendar
SEQUENCE:0
CREATED:20260821T144100Z
DESCRIPTION:It is a cliché to say that the brain is a complex system\, bu
 t it is: a network of billions of diverse units interacting across spatial
  and temporal scales spanning many orders of magnitude. Nevertheless\, bra
 ins are amenable to methods from physics to find lower-dimensional variabl
 es that capture the essence of many of the quantities we can measure. This
  talk will present an overview of mean field models of large-scale brain d
 ynamics\, in particular neural mass models and neural field theory. These 
 explain a wide range of both healthy and pathological brain dynamics. Some
  more recent applications include brain waves that spontaneously reconfigu
 re over time\, and how models can be used to explore potential treatments 
 for brain disorders.\n\nhttps://smp.uq.edu.au/event/session/17540
LAST-MODIFIED:20260821T144100Z
LOCATION:Physiology Lecture Theatres (63)\, Room: 358
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Extending density functional theory — Associate Professor Tim Go
 uld (Griffith University)
DTSTART:20260508T010000Z
DTEND:20260508T020000Z
DTSTAMP:20260821T144100Z
UID:17539-20260508@uq-seminar-calendar
SEQUENCE:0
CREATED:20260821T144100Z
DESCRIPTION:Density Functional Theory (DFT) is the workhorse of electronic
  structure theory calculations across physics\, chemistry\, engineering an
 d biology. Its popularity stems from reasonably accurate\, yet cheap\, app
 roximations that enable prediction of complex chemical systems. I will fir
 st introduce the theory behind DFT\, to highlight the exact results that u
 nderpin popular approximations. I will then discuss my own work on 'ensemb
 lisation' that extends the core theory and approximations into new regimes
  of electronic matter - especially excited state physics.\n\nhttps://smp.u
 q.edu.au/event/session/17539
LAST-MODIFIED:20260821T144100Z
LOCATION:Physiology Lecture Theatres (63)\, Room: 358
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Amelia Frank / Superconductivity — Dr Peter Jacobson (University
  of Queensland)
DTSTART:20260501T010000Z
DTEND:20260501T020000Z
