BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Impact of Core-Collapse Supernovae on Gravitational-Wave Astro
 physics — Dr Jade Powell (Swinburne University)
DTSTART:20260424T010000Z
DTEND:20260424T020000Z
DTSTAMP:20260821T144100Z
UID:17614-20260424@uq-seminar-calendar
SEQUENCE:0
CREATED:20260821T144100Z
DESCRIPTION:Core-collapse supernovae (CCSNe) mark the violent deaths of ma
 ssive stars and are among the most energetic explosions in the Universe. T
 hese catastrophic events are responsible for the formation of the neutron 
 stars and black holes observed through gravitational waves. In recent year
 s\, CCSNe have emerged as key targets for gravitational-wave detection\, o
 ffering a unique window into the central mechanism that powers these explo
 sions. This talk will discuss the latest developments in CCSN numerical si
 mulations\, including the impact on the birth properties of black holes an
 d neutron stars observed through gravitational waves\, the latest CCSN gra
 vitational waveform models\, and the potential for current and next-genera
 tion gravitational-wave detectors to extract astrophysical and nuclear phy
 sics insights from these events.\n\nhttps://smp.uq.edu.au/event/session/17
 614
LAST-MODIFIED:20260821T144100Z
LOCATION:Physiology Lecture Theatres\, Room: 358
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Momentum and mass entanglement with ultracold metastable helium at
 oms — Dr Sean Hodgman (The Australian National University)
DTSTART:20260417T010000Z
DTEND:20260417T020000Z
DTSTAMP:20260821T144100Z
UID:17613-20260417@uq-seminar-calendar
SEQUENCE:0
CREATED:20260821T144100Z
DESCRIPTION:Nonlocal entanglement between pair-correlated particles is a h
 ighly counter-intuitive aspect of quantum mechanics. While the rigorous Be
 ll’s inequality framework has enabled the demonstration of such entangle
 ment in photons and atomic internal states\, no experiment has yet involve
 d motional states of massive particles. Here we report the experimental ob
 servation of nonlocal correlations in motional states of momentum-entangle
 d ultracold helium atoms\, with entanglement generated via s-wave collisio
 ns. Using a Rarity-Tapster interferometer and a Bell-test framework\, we o
 bserve atom-atom correlations sufficient to exclude a range of local hidde
 n variable theories. This system could be extended to an entangled state o
 f different mass atoms generated between 3He* and 4He* collisions\, and po
 tentially open new avenues for studying gravitational effects in quantum s
 tates.\n\nhttps://smp.uq.edu.au/event/session/17613
LAST-MODIFIED:20260821T144100Z
LOCATION:Physiology Lecture Theatres\, Room: 358
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Cosmic relics in the Lab: what nuclear spins can tell us about the
  early Universe — Giovanni Pierobon (University of NSW)
DTSTART:20260327T010000Z
DTEND:20260327T020000Z
DTSTAMP:20260821T144100Z
UID:17537-20260327@uq-seminar-calendar
SEQUENCE:0
CREATED:20260821T144100Z
DESCRIPTION:The Universe's earliest relics\, such as the Cosmic Neutrino B
 ackground and dark matter\, have long remained beyond experimental reach. 
 In this talk\, I will show how state-of-the-art nuclear spin experiments o
 ffer a new and promising window into wave-like dark matter\, while achievi
 ng a competitive sensitivity to cosmic neutrinos with significant discover
 y potential in future implementations.\n\nhttps://smp.uq.edu.au/event/sess
 ion/17537
LAST-MODIFIED:20260821T144100Z
LOCATION:Building 69\, Room: 305
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
